  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA cold front has moved through the area but the 500mb trough\nwill rotate through the area today, bringing instability \nshowers to the entire area this afternoon. The bulk of the\nshowers will diminish after sunset but some could linger in far\neastern parts of the CWA as the flow becomes more northerly and\nscattered showers are expected to drop south of Lake Michigan to\nalong the IL/IN border during the evening. \n\nCold air advection is occurring behind the front, tightening \nthe pressure gradient. This is bringing gusty winds to the area \nearly this morning. Wind gusts over 30 mph have already occurred\nin some spots and gusts shows 80-90% chance that gusts over 30 \nwill continue through the day. In fact, the HREF shows a 40-60% \nchance of gusts over 35 mph east of I-55 during the afternoon \nand early evening hours. Winds will then decrease in the west \nduring the evening but not taper off in the east until around \nmidnight. \n\nCooler, fall like temperatures will advect into the area behind\nthe departing cold front this weekend. Highs today and Saturday\nwill be in the 50s, with lows in the mid to upper 30s both Sat \nand Sun morning. With skies becoming clear tonight and winds \nbecoming much lighter overnight, frost is expected to develop \nacross west central and parts of central Illinois late tonight. \nThe HREF is showing a 40-60% chance of temperatures dropping \nbelow 32 degrees northwest of the IL River late tonight/Sat \nmorning, so some low spots could see a light freeze. Frost is \npossible again Sunday morning, mainly in areas east of I-55 \nwhere the HREF is showing a 30-50% chance of temperatures \ndropping below 32 degrees. Because of the frost potential\ntonight, a frost advisory has been issued for areas west of a\nBloomington to Decatur to Shelbyville line. Another frost\nadvisory will likely be needed east of I-55 Saturday night.\nTemperatures will begin to warm starting Sunday, reaching into\nthe 60s. The rest of the extended forecast, Monday through \nFriday, looks to be above normal with highs in the 70s to around\n80, especially Thursday and Friday. Dry weather is also \nexpected through the extended period.",
